      <h1>Integration Governance &amp; Monitoring</h1>
      <p class="guide-dek">Design bi-directional syncs with clear ownership, logging, alerts, and rollback so connected systems stay trusted.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main id="main-content" class="guide-main" style="--accent:#06245B;--accent-soft:#F1F5F9;--accent-border:#CBD5E1;">
    <div class="container guide-layout">
      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field guide</div>
        <h2>The practical framework</h2>
        <p class="intro">An integration is part of your operating model, not a plug-in you set and forget. Governance gives every data flow a purpose, owner, contract, and recovery plan. Monitoring then turns silent failures into visible, actionable work.</p>
        <section><h3>Give every integration a contract</h3><p>Before building a sync, document what should move, in which direction, under what conditions, and who owns the result. Define the source of truth field by field. This eliminates the ambiguity that causes systems to overwrite each other.</p><ul><li>List objects, fields, direction, frequency, and transformation rules.</li><li>Define the unique key and the behavior for creates, updates, deletes, and replays.</li><li>Name a business owner and a technical owner for every integration.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Monitor the data, not just the connection</h3><p>A green connection does not mean the business process is healthy. Monitor record counts, latency, error rates, rejected values, and business exceptions. Alert on conditions that require action instead of sending noise that teams learn to ignore.</p><ul><li>Track successful, failed, skipped, and retried records.</li><li>Set thresholds for volume drops, sync delays, and validation failures.</li><li>Send alerts to an owned queue with the context needed to troubleshoot.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Build rollback and change control</h3><p>Every integration needs a safe way to pause, replay, and reverse a bad change. Keep a change log, test new mappings with a controlled sample, and schedule changes with a clear communication plan.</p><ul><li>Maintain a field mapping and version history.</li><li>Use a pause switch and documented rollback steps.</li><li>Test in a sandbox or limited segment before a full release.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>Implementation checklist</h3>
          <p>Use this checklist to turn the framework into a working next step.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>System of record documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Field map versioned</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Unique key confirmed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Error queue assigned</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Latency and volume monitored</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Rollback tested</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Trust comes from predictability. When teams know what moves, when it moves, and what happens when it fails, integrations become an advantage instead of a risk.</p></div>
